  "body": "## Confirmed behavior\n\n`chat.history` declares `count` as an optional unsigned integer in `pkgs/id/crates/id-core/src/world_caps.rs:258-263`. However, `parse_wants` only bounds the serialized `args` size (`world_caps.rs:798-805`); it does not validate the argument against that schema. The handler then reads `count` with `as_u64().unwrap_or(16)` (`pkgs/id/src/world/actor.rs:880-888`).\n\nFor example, this request is accepted and silently uses the default 16 instead of reporting invalid arguments:\n\n```json\n{\"v\":1,\"requests\":[{\"id\":\"h\",\"cap\":\"chat.history\",\"args\":{\"count\":\"2\"}}]}\n```\n\nThe same fallback applies to other non-unsigned values such as negative numbers, fractions, `null`, and objects. The existing test `chat_history_defaults_and_clamps_and_records_read_rejects_an_oversized_first_record` (`pkgs/id/tests/world/tests.rs:1782-1832`) verifies the omitted-count default and oversized-count clamp, but not malformed supplied values.\n\nThis is distinct from #796 / PR #4681: that proving test covers the default, clamp, and oversized `records.read` behavior, not wrong-typed `chat.history.count` values. No open PR for this behavior was found.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nA caller typo or serialization mismatch is silently turned into a successful request for 16 lines. The declared argument contract is therefore not enforced, making client errors harder to detect and potentially returning more history than requested.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- A supplied non-unsigned `chat.history.count` (at least string, negative, fractional, `null`, and object cases) is rejected with `bad_args`, rather than treated as omitted.\n- An omitted `count` still returns the documented default of 16.\n- Valid unsigned counts still work, and values above `MAX_CHAT_HISTORY_LINES` still clamp as they do today.\n- Add a world-level regression test exercising malformed input through the request path.\n",
